According to the web search results, the recommended syntax for defining classes in ES6 is as follows:

```
class ClassName {
  constructor(parameters) {
    this.property = value;
  }

  method(parameters) {
    return value;
  }
}
```

However, it is essential to note that ES6 classes are just syntactic sugar over the existing prototype-based model. They do not introduce any new functionality. Classes in JavaScript are essentially functions and objects, and the class syntax is only a more convenient way of expressing the same concepts.

Here's a more concise summary:

* Use the `class` keyword to define a class.
* Use the `constructor` method to initialize properties.
* Use methods to define functions that belong to the class.
* You can use the class syntax or the prototype-based model, as both serve the same purpose.

